Assistant: I see the issue. Instead of trying to mock the fonts using jest.mock (which won't work in this environment), we should create a wrapper component that provides mock font values. Let's modify the preview file:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/PageWrapper-707e5bc0.tsx">
<change>
<description>Replace font mocking with a wrapper component that provides mock font values</description>
<content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import ImportedComponent from '../../components/PageWrapper';

// Create a wrapper component that provides mock font values
const MockFontWrapper = ({ children }) => {
  // Inject the mock font styles directly
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const style = document.createElement('style');
    style.textContent = `
      :root {
        --font-inter: 'Inter', sans-serif;
        --font-cal: 'Cal Sans', sans-serif;
      }
    `;
    document.head.appendChild(style);
    return () => {
      document.head.removeChild(style);
    };
  }, []);

  return children;
};

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    Component: {
      type: 'object',
      value: {
        requiresLicense: false,
        isThemeSupported: true,
        isBookingPage: false,
        // Mock the getLayout function that PageWrapper expects
        getLayout: (page) => page,
      },
      label: 'Component',
    },
    pageProps: {
      type: 'object',
      value: {
        nonce: 'randomNonce123',
        themeBasis: 'light',
        session: {
          hasValidLicense: true,
          upId: 'user123',
          user: {
            id: 1,
            email: 'user@example.com',
            name: 'John Doe',
          },
        },
      },
      label: 'Page Props',
    },
    err: {
      type: 'object',
      value: null,
      label: 'Error',
    },
    router: {
      type: 'object',
      value: {
        pathname: '/home',
        asPath: '/home',
      },
      label: 'Router',
    },
  });

  return (
    <MockFontWrapper>
      <ImportedComponent
        Component={state.Component.value}
        pageProps={state.pageProps.value}
        err={state.err.value}
        router={state.router.value}
      />
    </MockFontWrapper>
  );
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

I've made the following changes:

1. Removed the jest.mock attempts since they won't work in this environment
2. Created a MockFontWrapper component that injects the required CSS variables directly into the document
3. Added the getLayout function to the Component mock since PageWrapper expects it
4. Wrapped the ImportedComponent with MockFontWrapper to provide the font variables

This approach should work better because:
1. It doesn't try to mock Next.js modules directly
2. It provides the necessary font variables through CSS
3. It properly handles the component lifecycle with useEffect
4. It includes all the necessary props that PageWrapper expects

The MockFontWrapper component injects the font variables directly into the document, which should allow the PageWrapper component to work without needing the actual Next.js font implementations.

